<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">La Navidad</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1492">1492</a>–<a href="/years/1493">1493</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">La Navidad was the first European settlement in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>, built on the north coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispaniola">Hispaniola</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christopher-columbus">Christopher Columbus</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christmas">Christmas</a> 1492 from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timbers</a> of the wrecked Santa María. Columbus left some 39 men there, in the territory of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taino">Taíno</a> chief <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guacanagari">Guacanagarí</a> of Marién. When he returned in late 1493 the fort had been destroyed and its men killed, an attack attributed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caonabo">Caonabo</a> of Maguana.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>La Navidad stood on the northwest coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispaniola">Hispaniola</a>, near present-day Caracol in the Nord-Est Department of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haiti">Haiti</a>. On Christmas Eve 1492, during <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christopher-columbus">Columbus</a>'s first voyage, the Santa María drifted onto a bank in Acul Bay and heeled over. Told by Guacanagari that there was much gold on the island, Columbus decided to leave the crew of the wreck to settle there and gather it, and had the ship taken apart to build a tower, a fortress and a large cellar. He named the harbour Puerto de la Navidad, Christmas Port.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christopher-columbus">Columbus</a> put the fort under Diego de Arana, chief constable of the fleet, Pedro Gutiérrez, butler of the royal dais, and Rodrigo de Escobedo. The men left behind included carpenters, caulkers, a physician, a tailor and a gunner, and the post was supplied with provisions, trade goods, arms and artillery. In his journal Columbus wrote that the islanders were naked, unarmed and very cowardly, and that his men could subjugate the whole island. On 4 January 1493 he sailed in the Niña in search of the Pinta, which had been missing for six weeks, and after finding it the fleet set out for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>.</p><p>On his second voyage <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christopher-columbus">Columbus</a> reached the site on 27 November 1493 and found the fort burned to the ground, with the bodies of eight Christians in nearby fields. He learned that the men had quarrelled among themselves, each taking women and gold. Gutiérrez and Escobedo had left with nine others for the gold mines, where the lord of the mines killed them before marching on Navidad, held by Arana with ten others and Guacanagari. There <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caonabo">Caonabo</a> set fire to the houses and drove Columbus's men into the sea, where eight drowned, while three were killed on shore. Columbus then built a new settlement farther east, in the present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominican-republic">Dominican Republic</a>, and named it <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-isabela">La Isabela</a> after Queen Isabella I.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The site was apparently forgotten until 1977, when a Haitian farmer led William Hodges, an American medical missionary and amateur archaeologist, to a spot in the marshland. With the permission of the Haitian government he excavated a section the size of a tennis court and found artefacts of La Navidad, but no conclusive evidence of its exact location has yet been found.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “La Navidad” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>From its timbers Columbus built the fort of <b>La Navidad</b>, the first European settlement in the Americas, in the territory of the Taíno chief Guacanagarí, and left some 39 men there.</blockquote><a href="/essays/christmas-day-in-history">Christmas Day in History</a></li><li><blockquote>Caonabo destroyed the Spanish fort of <b>La Navidad</b> in 1493.</blockquote><a href="/state/cacicazgo-of-maguana">Cacicazgo of Maguana</a></li><li><blockquote>Its cacique Guacanagarí helped the Spanish salvage the wreck of the Santa María on Christmas Day 1492, and Columbus built the fort of <b>La Navidad</b> with its timbers in his territory.</blockquote><a href="/state/cacicazgo-of-marien">Cacicazgo of Marién</a></li></ul></section>
